> If I had to decide somewhere to split, it would be right before allocating the netbuf. What do you think about that ?

Sounds like a fine place to do it. 

w.r.t. splitting the function, we can also do something like

```c

static bool gatt_notify_multiple_valid_args(...)
{
    ...
}

static bool gatt_notify_multiple_valid_params(...)
{
    ...
}

static struct net_buf *gatt_notify_multiple_create_pdu(...)
{
    ...
}

int bt_gatt_notify_multiple(struct bt_conn *conn, uint16_t num_params,
			    struct bt_gatt_notify_params *params)
{
    if (gatt_notify_multiple_valid_args()) {
        return -EINVAL;
    }

    if (gatt_notify_multiple_valid_params()) {
        return -EINVAL;
    }

    buf = gatt_notify_multiple_create_pdu();
    if (buf == NULL) {
        return -EINVAL;
    }
        
    return gatt_notify_mult_send(conn, buf);
}
```

Or have the functions return `int` (errno) values instead. 

The point is that we can easily add multiple functions for parameter validation etc., and if they are only used once (like they will here) they are going to be inlined by the compiler and thus we shouldn't see any performance/memory difference.
